#**********************************************************************#
#  ME 697 class project with a reactive Material X: StochasticIgnite   #
#                                                                      #
#  The class problem (me697_double_shock_2p00.deck), with Material X   #
#  burning by StochasticIgnite, the fourth of bitwhiz's burn models    #
#  (doc/design/burn_models.md): proposed by ChatGPT 4o-mini in         #
#  SAND2025-09228, repaired, and extended. Once a cell's lead shock is #
#  marked, its Avrami clock phi runs at a noisy rate,                  #
#                                                                      #
#   dphi/dt = max(MU + SIGMA Z, 0) (1 + P_s/PR)^NS,                    #
#   lam = 1 - exp(-phi^AVRAMI),                                        #
#                                                                      #
#  where Z, a standard normal, is shared by the cells of one bin of    #
#  initial position (LC wide) and wanders in time with correlation     #
#  time TAUZ (an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process). Bin j's Z starts from    #
#  the atmospheric-noise table (module random), entry                  #
#  modulo(SEED + 7919 j, 20000) + 1. The same SEED gives the same run, #
#  bit for bit; another SEED, another sample of the same material.     #
#                                                                      #
#      | Ta 3.0 | Al 4.0 |=> Material X 10.0 | PMMA 10.0 |  PDV ->     #
#     x=-7.0           x=0 (impact plane)    x=10.0      x=20.0        #
#                                                                      #
#  MU, SIGMA, AVRAMI, NS, LC and TAUZ here are an example, not a       #
#  calibration (that is the design note's step 10): chosen so that at  #
#  2.00 km/s the shock grows to a detonation about 3.4 mm into         #
#  Material X on average, near HVRB's 3.5 (me697_hvrb_2p00.deck), with #
#  a spread from SEED to SEED. The products are the HVRB deck's ideal  #
#  gas. The burn's keys, and what each does: input/README.md, Material #
#  block; every other key is the class deck's (its key reference, at   #
#  its end). Units: mm, us, g/cc, km/s, GPa, K.                        #
#**********************************************************************#
Begin bitwhiz
 deck_version = 1
 define u_fly = 2.00            # km/s, flyer velocity

 Begin Run
  plots_only = no    # yes or no
  title       = sign_2p00
  t_end       = 3.0             # us: the detonation reaches the PDV
                                # interface near 1.5 us at 2.00 km/s
  num_threads = 1
 End Run

 Begin Output                   # nothing listed here = nothing written
  plot_dt     = 0.005           # us between snapshots: under a
                                # pixel row of the t-x diagrams
  movie_frames = 35             # the pressure movie, 0 to 3.0 us
  tracer_dt   = 0.005           # us between tracer rows (the
                                # reference's spacing); 0 = every cycle
  colormap    = viridis         # for bitmap pictures

  tracer = pdv     MatX:PMMA    # the PDV signal
  tracer = impact  Al:MatX      # impact plane
  tracer = ta_al   Ta:Al        # where the second shock is born
  tracer = mid_x   MatX@0.5     # mid-sample, past the run distance
 End Output

 Begin Solver
  method = RS                   # the Riemann solver: a shock 1-2 cells
  order  = 2                    # sharp, and a smooth burn behind it
                                # (second order, MUSCL-Hancock)
  BL     = 0.1                  # method = AV's linear and quadratic
  BQ     = 2.0                  # viscosity coefficients (the equation
                                # sheet's defaults); RS ignores them
  CFL    = 0.6
 End Solver

 # Mie-Gruneisen on the principal Hugoniot (rev-0, Eqs. 4 and 6):
 #   Us = C0 + S1*up + (S2/C0)*up^2,   rho*Gamma = R0*G0
 Begin Material Ta              # tantalum, backing plate
  R0 = 16.65                    # g/cm^3, initial density
  C0 = 3.40                     # km/s, Hugoniot intercept
  S1 = 1.20                     # -,    Us-up slope
  S2 = 0.0                      # -,    Us-up curvature (default 0)
  G0 = 1.60                     # -,    Gruneisen coefficient
  spall_strength = 5.0          # GPa, dynamic. Tantalum is strong in
                                # tension (spall strengths of several
                                # GPa are reported); at 5.0 the Ta here
                                # holds its ~2 GPa tension, as in the
                                # reference data. Other materials take
                                # the default, 0.01 GPa (input/README.md)
 End Material

 Begin Material Al              # aluminum, flyer face
  R0 = 2.78
  C0 = 5.30
  S1 = 1.34
  S2 = 0.0
  G0 = 2.00
 End Material

 Begin Material MatX            # notional HE surrogate, reactive
  eos  = mie_gruneisen_t        # the unreacted solid, plus T: the burn's
  R0   = 1.60                   # closure mixes it with the products at
  C0   = 2.50                   # one density and one temperature
  S1   = 1.80
  S2   = -0.10
  G0   = 1.20
  CV   = 1.1633418              # J/(g K), both phases
  T0   = 298.0                  # K

  burn   = stochasticignite     # StochasticIgnite, gated by the lead shock
  MU     = 1.35                 # 1/us, the mean rate of the Avrami clock
  SIGMA  = 0.7                  # 1/us, the rate's noise amplitude
  AVRAMI = 3.0                  # Avrami exponent: lam = 1 - exp(-phi^3)
  NS     = 2.0                  # exponent on (1 + P_s/PR)
  PR     = 7.0                  # GPa, reference pressure
  LC     = 0.5                  # mm, the noise's correlation length:
                                # bins of initial position, from x = 0
  SEED   = 108                  # which sample: 0 to 19999, whole
  TAUZ   = 0.2                  # us, the noise's correlation time
                                # (1.0e30: each bin's Z frozen)

  products = ideal_gas          # P = (GAMMA - 1) rho CV T, as the HVRB
                                # deck's
  GAMMA    = 2.60
  Q        = 4.8828             # kJ/g (= MJ/kg), heat of reaction
 End Material

 Begin Material PMMA            # window; Lacina et al. (2018) linear fit
  R0 = 1.187
  C0 = 2.55
  S1 = 1.54
  S2 = 0.0
  G0 = 0.90                     # estimated from Plexiglass (rev-0 Table 1)
 End Material

 Begin Stack
  resolution = 35               # zones/mm: 105 + 140 + 350 + 350 = 945
  origin     = Al:MatX          # x = 0 at the impact plane

  Begin Layer
   material = Ta
   width    = 3.0               # mm
   xvel     = u_fly             # km/s, + is to the right
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = Al
   width    = 4.0
   xvel     = u_fly
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = MatX
   width    = 10.0
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = PMMA
   width    = 10.0
  End Layer
 End Stack
End bitwhiz

Notes (ignored by the parser):
- Seeds and the Pop plot, measured at this deck's 35 zones/mm, RS order
  2: at 2.00 km/s, over 16 seeds, the detonation runs 3.4 mm in on
  average (standard deviation 0.5 mm, 2.75 to 4.65 mm; SEED = 108, here,
  3.45 mm). Change "SEED" for another sample. Change "define u_fly" for
  the trend, over 8 seeds: 1.75 km/s 4.0 mm, 1.50 4.4 mm, 1.25 4.8 mm.
  The model has no threshold, as the survey proposed it: every marked
  cell burns at a rate set by P_s only through (1 + P_s/PR)^NS, so its
  Pop plot is shallow, and every speed tried detonates within 10 mm.
  Calibration (step 10) would choose the constants against HVRB's.
- The detonation runs 2 to 4 % below D_CJ (the ensemble's speed over 6 to
  9.5 mm, tests/verification/study_sign.py): a bin whose rate is low when
  the front reaches it burns behind the front, too late to drive it. With
  SIGMA = 0 the same deck runs at D_CJ.
- Each tracer's XRN column (the tracer file) is its cell's extent of
  reaction, lam, 0 to 1, 0 until its lead shock is marked.
- The lead shock: a cell's clock runs only after its lead shock is marked
  (SURF's detector, Menikoff and Shaw 2010; doc/design/burn_models.md,
  step 4), at P_s, frozen there. Z runs from t = 0 in every cell, marked
  or not, so a bin's Z is the same in all its cells.
- The noise: max(MU + SIGMA Z, 0) is never negative (a cell does not
  unburn), so where MU + SIGMA Z < 0 the clock stops until Z wanders back.
  With TAUZ = 1.0e30 Z never moves: a bin with MU + SIGMA Z <= 0 never
  burns (about 3 % of bins here). There, two SEEDs can give one field
  shifted by whole bins (SEED + 7919 starts bin j where SEED starts bin
  j + 1, 0.5 mm over); with TAUZ finite, the later draws, keyed by SEED
  itself, differ. Steps are sub-cycled to at most TAUZ/10.
  The white-noise limit, TAUZ to 0, is outside the model (the design
  note, step 7: the floor makes it diverge).
- SIGMA = 0 is a clock, lam = 1 - exp(-(MU S t)^AVRAMI); MU = SIGMA = 0
  the inert limit (the detector and Z still run, and change nothing).
- "Cells burnt out" on the screen counts the cells with lam within 1e-12
  of 1, for every model.
